# ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
# taxonomy.yaml — project extension of the classification scheme
# ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
#
# Location: vault/projects/{project-name}/_meta/taxonomy.yaml
#
# This file inherits the global taxonomy and adds the project's own classification.
# The category structure, document types, tag namespaces, lifecycle
# and validation rules defined globally are inherited as they are.
#
# Merge rules:
#   - categories.{name}.children → deep merge (add only, no removal)
#   - document_types              → may add; existing types cannot be redefined
#   - tag_namespaces              → may add
#   - validation                  → may override (relax or tighten per project)
#   - other fields                → keep the global value
# ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

version: "1.0"
scope: "project"
extends: "../../_meta/taxonomy.yaml"
project: "{project-name}"             # project identifier


# ============================================================
# Category extension — define domain children
# ============================================================
#
# The global taxonomy leaves categories.domain.children empty,
# so each project defines its domain topics here.
#
# Note: these children are the "directory axis" (where documents sit).
# The "topic nodes" that make up the human-readable table of contents have their own source of truth, _meta/toc.yaml,
# and do not map 1:1 to directories. You may give both the same names, but nothing enforces it.
#
# Structure:
#   domain/{topic}/                  depth 2
#   domain/{topic}/{subtopic}/       depth 3 (depth_limit: 3)
#
# Example (an order project):
#
# categories:
#   domain:
#     children:
#       order:
#         label: "Orders"
#         order: 1
#         children:
#           payment:
#             label: "Payment"
#             order: 1
#           refunds:
#             label: "Refunds"
#             order: 2
#           reporting:
#             label: "Reporting"
#             order: 3
#       organization:
#         label: "Organization / stores"
#         order: 2
#         children:
#           stores:
#             label: "Stores"
#             order: 1
#           warehouses:
#             label: "Warehouses"
#             order: 2
#
#   references:
#     children:
#       payment-standards:
#         label: "Payment standards"
#         order: 1
#       consumer-law:
#         label: "E-commerce law"
#         order: 2

categories:
  domain:
    children: {}                    # TODO: define the project's domain topics


# ============================================================
# Project-specific tags (optional)
# ============================================================
#
# Add project-specific tags here if the global namespaces are not enough.
#
# Example:
# tag_namespaces:
#   module:
#     description: "Module within the project"
#     pattern: "module/{name}"
#     examples: ["module/payment", "module/report", "module/admin"]


# ============================================================
# Validation rule overrides (optional)
# ============================================================
#
# A project may relax or tighten the global rules to fit its situation.
#
# Example (tightening in a settled project):
# validation:
#   required_fields_check:
#     action: "block"              # tightened from warn to block
#   category_type_check:
#     action: "block"
